    description: "FOUND 2026-10-22 by the WI-lanos re-measure (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lanos_rerun_10212026/RESULT.md; adjudication_part3.md rows 5, 6, 20), also seen on 10-03 (run_10032026/lanos/RESULT.md: 3 of 60). Owner asked for this to be tracked on its own: none of WI-lanos's options fixes it.\n\nTHE GAP. `subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True)` / `stdout=subprocess.PIPE` returns a CompletedProcess whose `.stdout` / `.stderr` IS the child's bytes, a transfer under ADR-0049 Ruling 1. No row mints it. python.yaml (the `functions: [run, Popen, call, check_call]` launch row, INV-lozat's note) says why: \"the catalogue matches on name, not on keyword arguments. Rowing it would mint a source at every subprocess.run call in the corpus, most of which capture nothing -- so it stays out until the argument shape can be read\". The omission is disclosed, but the flows are real:\n  qemu-meson mesonbuild/scripts/yasm.py:18 subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True) -> :22 f.write(ret.stdout)\n  qemu-meson mesonbuild/scripts/tags.py:13 git ls-tree .stdout (callee ls_as_bytestream) -> :24\n  qemu-meson tools/regenerate_docs.py:25 .stdout via _get_meson_output (:102)\ncheck_output carries the same bytes and does mint. So the same data gets two verdicts depending on which API fetched it: the WI-lanos inconsistency, on output instead of status.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (design, not decided):\n  (a) An argument-conditional row: mint ipc_recv at the call only when the call site passes capture_output=True, or stdout= / stderr= equal to subprocess.PIPE. First check whether any existing catalogue machinery reads call arguments. open()'s mode-dependent fs_read/fs_write \"dual-classified\" notes may be prose only; that is unverified.\n  (b) Anchor the source at the attribute read: `.stdout` / `.stderr` on a value bound to a subprocess.run result.\n(a) is closer to how rows work today; (b) needs receiver typing of the CompletedProcess. Prefer whichever does NOT mint at a call that captures nothing, since that is the cost INV-lozat refused.\nSiblings to check in the same change: asyncio.create_subprocess_exec / _shell with stdout=PIPE, then .communicate(); Popen(stdout=PIPE).stdout.read(), which may already be represented as a handle read (check, do not assume).\n\nACCEPTANCE. Production CLI, cold cache, untrusted-input-no-subprocess (or the generic taint claims): a fixture `out = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True).stdout; os.system(out)` gives violated, and its check_output twin gives the same verdict. A control `subprocess.run(cmd)` (no capture) mints nothing. Measure the marginal delta on a cohort before merging (the 0003 method); the yasm.py row above is a positive control."  # 659c
    fields: {}  # 659c
